  "live_records": {
    "q-170657": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170657",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ compute $P(X=1).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
