    "q-97190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97190",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.5)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.3)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.5,\\,P(X=0)=0.5,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.3),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.3,\\,P(Y=0)=0.7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & =0.5\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
