    "q-97157": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97157",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.6)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.6)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0).$",
      "worked_solution": "By the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1\\,\\cap \\,Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.6),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.6,\\,P(X=0)=0.4,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.6),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.6,\\,P(Y=0)=0.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & =0.6+0.4-0.6\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1-0.24 \\\\  & =0.76.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
