    "q-170665": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170665",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.9)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.48)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0).$",
      "worked_solution": "By the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.9),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.9,\\,P(X=0)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.48),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.48,\\,P(Y=0)=0.52.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.52-0.1\\cdot 0.52 \\\\  & =0.62-0.052 \\\\  & =0.568.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
