    "q-97166": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97166",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.1)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.8)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0).$",
      "worked_solution": "By the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.1,\\,P(X=0)=0.9,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.8),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.8,\\,P(Y=0)=0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=0)+P(Y=0)-P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & =0.9+0.2-0.9\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =1.1-0.18 \\\\  & =0.92.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
