    "q-97153": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97153",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.4)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.2)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0).$",
      "worked_solution": "By the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1\\,\\cap \\,Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cap \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.4),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.4,\\,P(X=0)=0.6,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.2,\\,P(Y=0)=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\cup \\,Y=0) & =P(X=1)+P(Y=0)-P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & =0.4+0.8-0.4\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =1.2-0.32 \\\\  & =0.88.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
